<h2>Consent, TCPA, and state privacy rules</h2>
<p>
Every promotional text your store sends needs express written consent tied to a specific phone number, a disclosure of message types and frequency, and a retrievable record of the opt-in event. Under the TCPA and state privacy laws such as CCPA/CPRA, Colorado, Virginia, and Washington, the store — not the app — carries the liability for consent evidence, STOP latency, and quiet-hour enforcement.
</p>
<p>
Before enabling SMS for {topic.audience.toLowerCase()}, verify four things in the tool you choose: consent is stored separately from email consent with source and timestamp; STOP suppresses across every flow within seconds; HELP routes to a readable reply; and quiet hours resolve to the recipient's local time zone. Purchase history never substitutes for SMS permission — a buyer who gave an email at checkout has not consented to texts.
</p>
<p>
If your existing consent ledger cannot prove source, timestamp, and message-type scope, re-permission with a keyword campaign and an unambiguous disclosure instead of importing the audience on hope. That week of slower growth buys a consent artifact that survives carrier filtering and complaint review.
</p>

<h2>Purchaser suppression and flow collisions</h2>
<p>
The most damaging SMS failure is not a bad message — it is a mistimed one. A cart reminder after checkout completes, a winback text the day a renewal charges, or a launch alert sent to a customer with an open support ticket each burn trust and invite opt-outs. Build a suppression matrix before you build flows: which events exit a journey instantly (order created, subscription renewed), which delay it (refund pending), and which branch it (gift buyer vs. the buyer themself).
</p>
<p>
Then coordinate channels. If email and SMS both run cart recovery, one channel owns the first hour and the other becomes a documented fallback at 4–24 hours, with no duplicated offer inside a single intent window. Write the rule down; unowned defaults always collide eventually.
</p>

<h2>Margin math: what each text actually earns</h2>
<p>
Work an example before you scale. A $100 order at 55% gross margin, a 15% recovery discount, a three-touch cart flow at roughly $0.17 per message (segments plus carrier surcharge) leaves about $40 of contribution per recovered order. Swap in 25% off or MMS creative and the same flow earns closer to $25. Attribution dashboards will happily report the $100; only margin math tells you whether the program paid.
</p>
<p>
Track per flow: cost per delivered message, discount cost per incremental order, opt-outs per 100 delivered messages, and the 90-day repeat rate of the recovered cohort. Keep a holdout so "attributed revenue" never stands in for "incremental profit," and pause any flow whose opt-out rate rises without a matching contribution gain.
</p>

<h2>App costs and pricing: what to verify before you commit</h2>
<p>
SMS app pricing is a moving target. Plan names, contact tiers, per-message rates, MMS premiums, carrier surcharges, and regional availability all change, and vendor pricing pages do not always show overage terms or number-verification fees. Treat every number you read — including on this page — as a buying-model note, not a quote, and check the official pricing page of each shortlisted tool for current terms.
</p>
<p>
Request a written twelve-month estimate at your real volume: monthly sends, average segments per message, MMS share, expected list growth, and the countries you will text. Ask separately about onboarding fees, premium integrations, seat limits, and what happens to billing during a seasonal spike. For the full framework, see the <a href="/guides/pricing">SMS pricing guide</a>; for consent mechanics, the <a href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a> and <a href="/guides/tcpa">TCPA primer</a>; and for timing rules, <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet hours</a>.
</p>

<h2>Negotiation and vendor checklist before you sign</h2>
<p>
SMS vendors expect procurement questions at volume, so ask them. Request written confirmation of: per-message and MMS rates at your peak-month volume, carrier surcharge pass-throughs, whether undelivered or attempted messages bill, overage thresholds, seat and integration limits, onboarding and migration fees, data export on exit, and cancellation notice. A vendor that will not put these in writing has answered the question.
</p>
<p>
Ask, too, what the vendor's support includes at your plan tier: response times during a carrier incident, whether a human reviews consent configurations, and who owns fixing a misfiring trigger. Recovery and launch windows are exactly when misfires are most expensive.
</p>
<p>
Run the same negotiation with two finalists and let the written estimates compete. The differences usually appear in the second year — overage pricing, add-on features you assumed were included, and support response times during a launch or a carrier incident — not in the first invoice.
</p>

