<h2>FAQ</h2>
<h3>What should a Shopify SMS pilot include?</h3>
<p>One permissioned audience, one message job, a clear opt-out, quiet-hour rules, purchaser suppression, an owner for replies, and a holdout or baseline. Report incremental profit, not clicks alone.</p>
<h3>Should SMS and email share a frequency cap?</h3>
<p>They should share a customer-level contact policy even when the channels have separate operational owners. Otherwise a customer can receive an email and SMS for the same state without anyone seeing the collision.</p>
<h3>Is the pricing in this guide guaranteed?</h3>
<p>No. Plan names, contact tiers, per-message rates, and regional SMS availability change, and this page links each tool's official source for current terms. Request a written twelve-month estimate at your real volume before forecasting.</p>
<h3>How many SMS flows should a store start with?</h3>
<p>One or two. Cart recovery with purchaser suppression and a winback or replenishment reminder for repeatable products cover most of the value. Add launch alerts, back-in-stock, and VIP campaigns only after opt-out rates and margin math clear your thresholds for the first flows.</p>
<h3>Do I need a separate SMS provider if I already have an email tool?</h3>
<p>Often yes. Email-first platforms can plan and time the lifecycle, but carrier compliance, keyword acquisition, reply handling, and regional number provisioning usually belong with a dedicated SMS sender. Pair the two deliberately with a documented consent and collision map rather than forcing one tool to do both jobs poorly.</p>
<h3>What quiet hours should a Shopify SMS program run?</h3>
<p>A common starting window is 8 a.m. to 9 p.m. in the recipient's local time zone, tightened for time-sensitive industries and adjusted for state-level telemarketing rules. What matters operationally is that the window resolves per recipient — not per store — and that scheduled broadcasts are re-checked against the window at send time, not at schedule time.</p>
<h3>How do I measure whether SMS is actually working?</h3>
<p>Compare a held-out cohort against the messaged cohort and report incremental orders, incremental contribution margin after discounts and messaging fees, opt-out rate, and the 90-day repeat rate of the messaged cohort. If the platform cannot support a holdout or the increments do not clear your margin threshold, reduce frequency before you reduce spend.</p>
<p class="text-neutral-400">Related reading: <a href="/use-cases">Shopify messaging use cases</a>, <a href="/guides/consent">consent and compliance</a>, <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">email/SMS coordination</a>, and <a href="/alternatives">SMS alternatives</a>.</p>
